"""Study skills, career, wellness, money basics and everyday how-things-work."""

ROWS = [
    # ---- learning & productivity ----
    ("how to learn programming", "Learn programming by building small things you actually want: a script that renames your files, a page that shows your photos. Read just enough theory to unblock the build, expect errors as the normal state of affairs, and finish projects even when they are ugly. Finishing is the skill that compounds. 💻", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to learn programming", "how to learn to code", "how do i learn coding", "best way to learn programming", "how can i start coding"], "keys": ["learn", "programming"]}),
    ("how to stay focused", "Focus is mostly environmental: put the phone in another room, close the extra tabs, pick one task and define what \"done\" looks like for the next 25 minutes. Willpower is a bad strategy; friction is a good one. 🎯", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to stay focused", "how to focus better", "how do i concentrate", "cant focus", "how to improve focus", "how to stop getting distracted"], "keys": ["focus", "concentrate"]}),
    ("time management", "Time management is really priority management: list what actually matters this week, block calendar time for the top items before meetings eat the day, and say no more often than is comfortable. You cannot make more time; you can only spend it deliberately. ⏰", {"templ": False, "asks": ["time management", "how to manage time", "how to get more done", "how to be productive", "productivity tips"], "keys": ["time", "productive"]}),
    ("the pomodoro technique", "The Pomodoro technique: work in focused 25-minute blocks, take a 5-minute break, and after four blocks take a longer break. The real mechanism is starting — a 25-minute promise is small enough to keep, and momentum does the rest. 🍅", {"templ": False, "asks": ["pomodoro technique", "what is pomodoro", "pomodoro method"], "keys": ["pomodoro"]}),
    ("how to study effectively", "Study actively, not passively: close the book and try to recall what you just read, work practice problems, explain the idea out loud in plain words. Highlighting and re-reading feel productive and mostly are not. 📚", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to study", "how to study effectively", "study tips", "best way to study", "how to study for exams", "how to revise"], "keys": ["study", "studying"]}),
    ("spaced repetition", "Spaced repetition schedules reviews right before you would forget — day 1, day 3, day 7, day 30 — because each successful recall strengthens memory more than rereading ever does. Flashcard apps like Anki automate the schedule; the principle works with paper too. 🔁", {"templ": False, "asks": ["spaced repetition", "what is spaced repetition", "how to remember things long term"], "keys": ["repetition", "remember"]}),
    ("active recall", "Active recall is testing yourself instead of reviewing: cover the notes, retrieve the answer from memory, then check. It feels harder than rereading — that difficulty is the point, because retrieval is what builds durable memory. 🧠", {"templ": False, "asks": ["active recall", "what is active recall", "recall practice"], "keys": ["recall"]}),
    ("how to take notes", "Take notes in your own words, not transcriptions — the compression is where learning happens. Structure beats prettiness: Cornell-style columns, concept maps, or simple question-and-answer pairs you can later test yourself with. 📝", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to take notes", "note taking tips", "best note taking method", "how to take better notes"], "keys": ["notes", "notetaking"]}),
    ("how to stop procrastinating", "Procrastination is usually emotional, not lazy: the task feels big, vague, or scary, so you flinch. Shrink it — \"open the document and write one terrible sentence\" — and forgive the flinch. Starting is the whole battle. 🐢", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to stop procrastinating", "stop procrastination", "why do i procrastinate", "how to not procrastinate"], "keys": ["procrastination"]}),
    ("motivation and discipline", "Motivation is weather; discipline is climate. The reliable strategy is to make the right action small and automatic (habit) and the wrong action annoying (friction) — then you need far less of either. 💪", {"templ": False, "asks": ["motivation vs discipline", "how to be disciplined", "how to build discipline", "why motivation doesnt last"], "keys": ["discipline", "motivation"]}),
    ("how to build a habit", "Habits form from repetition in a stable context: pick a tiny version of the behavior, attach it to an existing routine (\"after coffee, I write one paragraph\"), and make it easy to do and easy to track. Consistency beats intensity for weeks before intensity matters at all. 🔄", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to build a habit", "habit formation", "how to form a habit", "how long to build a habit"], "keys": ["habit", "habits"]}),
    ("deep work", "Deep work is undistracted concentration on cognitively demanding tasks — the state that produces your best output. It needs protected blocks (90 minutes or more), a clear target, and zero notifications; most \"busy\" days never contain one. 🧘", {"templ": False, "asks": ["deep work", "what is deep work", "how to do deep work"], "keys": ["deepwork"]}),
    ("how to read a research paper", "Read a paper in passes: abstract and conclusion first, then figures, then methods — and stop as soon as the paper stops being relevant. Ask three questions: what problem, what did they do, what evidence. Most papers survive about ten minutes of this. 📄", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to read a research paper", "how to read papers", "reading research papers", "how to understand papers"], "keys": ["papers", "research"]}),
    ("how to cite sources", "Cite whenever you use someone else's words, data, or specific idea: quote with quotation marks and a source, paraphrase with a source, and keep a running bibliography as you work. Citation styles (APA, MLA, IEEE) differ; the honesty principle does not. 📚", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to cite sources", "how to cite", "citation styles", "how to reference sources"], "keys": ["cite", "citation"]}),
    ("plagiarism", "Plagiarism is presenting someone else's work or words as your own — including copy-pasted paragraphs, uncited paraphrase, and recycled essays. It is treated as a serious academic offense, and \"I forgot to cite\" stops being a defense the moment you submit. ⚠️", {"templ": False, "asks": ["what is plagiarism", "plagiarism definition", "is copying plagiarism"], "keys": ["plagiarism"]}),
    ("sleep hygiene", "Sleep works best on a schedule: same bedtime and wake time daily, dark cool room, no screens for the last stretch, and caffeine before noon if you are sensitive. Your brain treats sleep as maintenance time — skip it and everything else gets harder. 😴", {"templ": False, "asks": ["sleep hygiene", "how to sleep better", "tips for better sleep", "how to fall asleep faster", "insomnia tips"], "keys": ["sleep-hygiene"]}),
    ("exercise basics", "For general health, the evidence favors consistency over intensity: about 150 minutes of moderate activity a week (brisk walking counts) plus two strength sessions. The best exercise program is the one you will still be doing in six months. 🏃", {"templ": False, "asks": ["exercise basics", "how much should i exercise", "how to start exercising", "benefits of exercise", "how to get in shape"], "keys": ["exercise", "workout"]}),
    ("healthy eating", "Nutrition in one paragraph: mostly plants, enough protein, not too much sugar, and more home cooking than takeout. Dramatic diets mostly work because people pay attention — the boring habits are what sustain results. 🥗", {"templ": False, "asks": ["healthy eating", "how to eat healthy", "what is a healthy diet", "nutrition basics"], "keys": ["nutrition", "diet"]}),
    ("drinking water", "Water needs vary with body size, activity, and climate — a common rule of thumb is roughly 2 to 3 liters a day for adults from all sources, more when active or hot. Thirst and pale-yellow urine are decent live indicators. 💧", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how much water to drink", "how much water should i drink", "daily water intake", "is drinking water important"], "keys": ["water-intake"]}),
    ("meditation", "Meditation is attention training: sit, follow your breath, notice when the mind wanders, and gently return — the returning *is* the exercise. Even 10 minutes a day measurably reduces stress for many people, and the app you choose matters far less than doing it. 🧘", {"templ": False, "asks": ["meditation", "how to meditate", "benefits of meditation", "mindfulness"], "keys": ["meditate", "mindfulness"]}),
    ("stress management", "Stress management is mostly about recovery: physical activity, sleep, time with people you like, and naming what you can and cannot control. Chronic stress is a health issue, not a personality flaw — treat it with the same seriousness as a physical injury. 🧘", {"templ": False, "asks": ["stress management", "how to manage stress", "how to reduce stress", "how to deal with stress"], "keys": ["stress"]}),
    ("burnout", "Burnout is chronic workplace or study stress that has not been balanced by recovery — exhaustion, cynicism, feeling ineffective. It is not fixed by a weekend off; it usually needs changes to workload and boundaries, and professional support is a reasonable step, not a weakness. 🔥", {"templ": False, "asks": ["what is burnout", "burnout symptoms", "how to recover from burnout", "feeling burnt out"], "keys": ["burnout"]}),
    ("work life balance", "Work-life balance is not a 50/50 split — it is a sustainable allocation you can keep for years, with boundaries you actually enforce (notifications off, one day fully offline). The exact ratio changes with seasons; the sustainability is the point. ⚖️", {"templ": False, "asks": ["work life balance", "how to balance work and life", "healthy work life balance"], "keys": ["balance"]}),

    # ---- communication & career ----
    ("how to write an email", "A good email has a specific subject line, one clear ask near the top, the minimal context the reader needs, and a polite close. Assume the recipient reads it on a phone between meetings: shorter is kinder. 📧", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to write an email", "email writing tips", "how to write a professional email"], "keys": ["email-writing"]}),
    ("how to give a presentation", "Presentations are storytelling: one idea per slide, visuals over paragraphs, and rehearsal until the key transitions are automatic. Start with why the audience should care, end with what you want them to do — and cut 20% of the slides you planned. 🎤", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to give a presentation", "presentation tips", "how to present well", "how to make a presentation"], "keys": ["presentation", "presenting"]}),
    ("public speaking", "Public speaking anxiety shrinks with preparation and exposure: rehearse out loud, know your first 30 seconds cold, arrive early to own the room, and remember the audience wants you to succeed. Skill comes from repetitions, not from waiting to feel confident first. 🎙️", {"templ": False, "asks": ["public speaking", "how to speak in public", "fear of public speaking", "how to overcome stage fright"], "keys": ["speaking"]}),
    ("stage fright", "Stage fright is your body trying to help — same arousal as excitement, different label. Use it: slow breathing, planted feet, a prepared opening line, and eye contact with a friendly face. Nearly everyone who speaks well feels it; they just go first. 🎭", {"templ": False, "asks": ["stage fright", "how to deal with stage fright", "nervous before speaking"], "keys": ["nervous"]}),
    ("networking", "Networking is just making friendly, useful contact before you need it: ask people about their work, follow up with a specific thank-you, and give something (an answer, an introduction) before asking for anything. Depth beats breadth — ten real contacts outlast a thousand business cards. 🤝", {"templ": False, "asks": ["networking", "how to network", "networking tips", "how to make professional connections"], "keys": ["network"]}),
    ("how to write a resume", "A resume is a one-page argument that you can do the job: lead with results and numbers (\"cut build time 40%\"), use the job posting's vocabulary, keep formatting plain enough for both humans and parsers, and cut everything that does not support the argument. 📄", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to write a resume", "resume tips", "how to make a cv", "resume advice", "how to write a cv"], "keys": ["resume-writing", "cv"]}),
    ("how to prepare for an interview", "Interview prep: research the company's actual product, prepare 3–5 stories in situation-action-result shape, rehearse explaining your projects in one minute and one paragraph, and have two thoughtful questions ready. Practice out loud — reading silently is a different skill. 🎤", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to prepare for an interview", "interview tips", "how to pass an interview", "interview preparation"], "keys": ["interview"]}),
    ("internship advice", "For internships: apply widely and early (volume beats perfection), tailor the one page that matters, build one visible project you can talk about, and ask for referrals from alumni you have actually talked to. Rejection is the default outcome and the normal path. 💼", {"templ": False, "asks": ["internship advice", "how to get an internship", "how to find internships", "internship tips"], "keys": ["internship-advice"]}),
    ("how to choose a major", "Choose a major by triangulating three things: what you enjoy doing for hours, what you are demonstrably good at, and what doors it opens that you actually want. If unsure, bias toward the option that keeps more doors open — and remember the major matters less than the portfolio you build. 🎓", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to choose a major", "what major should i choose", "choosing a college major"], "keys": ["major"]}),
    ("how to ask for help", "Ask for help early, specifically, and with evidence of effort: \"I tried X and Y; the error is Z; could you point me at what to read?\" People love helping someone who is clearly in motion — and waiting too long is the more common mistake. 🙋", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to ask for help", "when to ask for help", "how to ask questions"], "keys": ["ask-help"]}),
    ("impostor syndrome", "Impostor syndrome is the gap between how much you know you do not know and how competent others perceive you. Nearly everyone competent feels it; the treatment is not denial but evidence — keep a running list of things you actually shipped, and reread it when the feeling spikes. 🧠", {"templ": False, "asks": ["impostor syndrome", "what is impostor syndrome", "feeling like a fraud", "do i belong here"], "keys": ["impostor"]}),
    ("a growth mindset", "A growth mindset is treating ability as trainable: \"I cannot do this yet\" instead of \"I am bad at this.\" The research is more nuanced than the slogan, but the practical core holds — feedback is information, mistakes are data, and practice with feedback is the mechanism. 🌱", {"templ": False, "asks": ["growth mindset", "what is growth mindset", "fixed vs growth mindset"], "keys": ["mindset"]}),
    ("how to make a decision", "For a real decision: write down the options, the criteria that matter, and how you would feel if each option failed. Sleep on it, then commit fully — the quality of the execution usually matters more than the last 10% of the choice. 🧭", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to make a decision", "decision making tips", "how to decide", "decision making process"], "keys": ["decide", "decision"]}),
    ("opportunity cost", "Opportunity cost is the value of the best thing you gave up — the true price of any choice is not just what it costs, but what else that time, money, or attention could have become. It is the single most useful idea in economics for everyday decisions. ⚖️", {"templ": False, "asks": ["opportunity cost", "what is opportunity cost"], "keys": ["opportunity-cost"]}),
    ("how to say no", "Say no to the request, not the person: \"Thanks for thinking of me — I cannot take this on right now without dropping something I have committed to.\" A clear no is kinder than a vague yes that quietly fails later. 🙅", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to say no", "how to decline politely", "saying no"], "keys": ["say-no"]}),
    ("how to apologize", "A real apology names the specific thing you did, acknowledges its impact, does not add \"but,\" and states what you will do differently. Cheap apologies defend the self-image; useful ones repair the relationship. 🙏", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to apologize", "how to say sorry", "how to apologize properly"], "keys": ["apologize"]}),
    ("negotiation", "Negotiation basics: know your best alternative if the deal fails, let the other side talk first if you can, anchor with a specific number backed by market data, and treat it as a joint problem, not a duel. Silence after your number is a tool, not an emergency. 🤝", {"templ": False, "asks": ["negotiation tips", "how to negotiate", "negotiation basics", "how to negotiate a job offer"], "keys": ["negotiate"]}),

    # ---- money basics ----
    ("compound interest", "Compound interest is interest earning interest: $100 at 7% becomes $200 in about 10 years, $400 in 20 — the curve bends harder the longer it runs. It is why starting early beats investing more later, and why debt compounds against you just as faithfully. 📈", {"templ": False, "asks": ["compound interest", "what is compound interest", "how does compound interest work"], "keys": ["compound"]}),
    ("how to budget", "A working budget is three numbers: what comes in, what must go out, and what you choose to save. Track everything for one month to see the truth, automate the savings transfer first, then give the rest a job — budgets fail from optimism, not from math. 💰", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to budget", "budgeting basics", "how to make a budget", "budgeting tips"], "keys": ["budget"]}),
    ("saving money", "Saving works best as a default, not a decision: move a fixed percentage out the day money arrives, build one month of expenses as a starter emergency fund, and re-evaluate big recurring costs once a year. You cannot out-willpower a bad system. 🏦", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to save money", "saving money tips", "how to save more money"], "keys": ["saving", "save-money"]}),
    ("an emergency fund", "An emergency fund is 3–6 months of essential expenses in boring, instantly accessible cash — it is what turns a layoff or a broken car from a crisis into an inconvenience. It is also the most reliably good financial move for most people, because it prevents panic debt. 🧯", {"templ": False, "asks": ["emergency fund", "what is an emergency fund", "how much emergency fund"], "keys": ["emergency-fund"]}),
    ("inflation", "Inflation is the general rise in prices over time, which means money quietly buys less. The US Federal Reserve targets about 2% a year; at that rate prices double in roughly 36 years — which is why cash under a mattress loses value and why wage growth should be compared against inflation. 📉", {"templ": False, "asks": ["what is inflation", "why is everything expensive", "how does inflation work"], "keys": ["inflation"]}),
    ("the stock market", "The stock market is where shares of companies trade: buying a share makes you a part-owner of a business's future profits. Prices move on expectations and emotion in the short run and on earnings in the long run — which is why patient diversified investing is the boring, evidence-backed default and day-trading is mostly a transfer of money to brokers. 📊", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how does the stock market work", "what is the stock market", "should i invest in stocks"], "keys": ["stocks", "market"]}),
    ("an index fund", "An index fund holds a whole market — like the S&P 500 — in one cheap, automatic package, so you own a slice of hundreds of companies and never pick winners. Decades of data show most professional stock-pickers underperform these funds after fees; that is why they are the standard default. 📈", {"templ": False, "asks": ["what is an index fund", "index funds explained", "etf vs index fund"], "keys": ["index-fund", "etf"]}),
    ("cryptocurrency", "Cryptocurrency is digital money recorded on a blockchain, with no central bank: Bitcoin started it in 2009 and thousands followed. It genuinely enables borderless transfers and programmable finance — and it is also volatile, partially fraudulent, and full of projects with no reason to exist. Know which one you are looking at. 🪙", {"templ": False, "asks": ["what is cryptocurrency", "what is crypto", "is crypto a good investment", "what is bitcoin"], "keys": ["crypto", "bitcoin"]}),
    ("blockchain", "A blockchain is a shared ledger maintained by many computers: transactions are grouped into blocks, cryptographically chained, and agreed by consensus rules, so no single party can rewrite history. It is genuinely useful for trustless coordination — and wildly oversold for problems that a normal database solves better. ⛓️", {"templ": False, "asks": ["what is blockchain", "how does blockchain work", "blockchain explained"], "keys": ["blockchain"]}),

    # ---- how things work ----
    ("the internet", "The internet is a network of networks: computers agree on protocols (TCP/IP), route packets between addresses, and the web — pages, links, HTTP — rides on top as one of many services. Nobody owns it; it works because everyone follows the same open standards. 🌐", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how does the internet work", "what is the internet", "who owns the internet", "who invented the internet"], "keys": ["internet"]}),
    ("wifi", "Wi-Fi carries internet data over radio waves between your device and a router, which connects onward to your ISP. It is shared airtime — more devices and walls mean less speed — which is why the wired connection is still the gamer's choice. 📶", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how does wifi work", "what is wifi"], "keys": ["wifi"]}),
    ("gps", "GPS works by trilateration: your receiver times signals from at least four satellites with atomic clocks, and the differences pin your position to within a few meters. The satellites broadcast time; your phone does the geometry — and relativity corrections are built into the system. 🛰️", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how does gps work", "what is gps"], "keys": ["gps"]}),
    ("a touchscreen", "A capacitive touchscreen senses the tiny electrical change your finger causes in a grid of transparent conductors — that is why it ignores gloves but registers skin. It reports coordinates many times per second, and software decides whether that was a tap, a swipe, or a scroll. 👆", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how does a touchscreen work", "how do touchscreens work"], "keys": ["touchscreen"]}),
    ("a battery", "A battery stores energy chemically and releases it as electricity: lithium-ion cells shuttle lithium ions between electrodes, and charging runs the reaction backwards. Capacity fades because the chemistry slowly degrades — heat and full charges speed it up. 🔋", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how does a battery work", "how do batteries work", "why do batteries die"], "keys": ["battery"]}),
    ("solar panels", "Solar panels are photovoltaic cells: photons knock electrons loose in a semiconductor sandwich, creating a current. They have no moving parts, last decades, and work best in bright indirect light — the panel on your roof is silently doing quantum mechanics all day. ☀️", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how do solar panels work", "how does solar power work"], "keys": ["solar"]}),
    ("how a microwave oven works", "A microwave oven generates radio waves at about 2.45 GHz that make water molecules rotate billions of times per second — molecular friction becomes heat. That is why it heats leftovers fast, does not brown food, and why metal (and sometimes nothing) sparks: it is electromagnetic engineering in a box. 🍲", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how does a microwave work", "how do microwaves heat food"], "keys": ["microwave"]}),
    ("noise cancelling headphones", "Noise-cancelling headphones listen to the outside world with tiny microphones, invert the incoming sound wave, and play the inversion — and the two waves cancel where they meet your ear. It works best on steady drone (engines, fans) and barely touches sudden voices. 🎧", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how does noise cancelling work", "how do noise cancelling headphones work"], "keys": ["noise-cancelling"]}),
    ("jet lag", "Jet lag happens when your internal clock disagrees with local time: eastward travel is usually worse because your body finds it harder to fall asleep earlier. Sunlight at the destination is the main lever — morning light advances your clock, evening light delays it. ✈️", {"templ": False, "asks": ["jet lag", "what is jet lag", "how to avoid jet lag"], "keys": ["jetlag"]}),
    ("why we yawn", "The honest answer: nobody fully knows. Best candidates are brain cooling and social contagion — yawning spreads between people who are paying attention to each other, and even reading the word can trigger one. You just yawned, probably. 🥱", {"templ": False, "asks": ["why do we yawn", "what causes yawning", "is yawning contagious"], "keys": ["yawn"]}),
    ("why the ocean is salty", "Rain dissolves mineral salts from rock and carries them to the sea; water evaporates, salt stays, and the cycle has repeated for billions of years. That is why the ocean is about 3.5% salt — and why a lake with no outlet slowly becomes saltier than the sea. 🌊", {"templ": False, "asks": ["why is the ocean salty", "why is the sea salty"], "keys": ["salty"]}),
    ("why leaves change color", "Leaves change color in autumn because shorter days trigger trees to stop making chlorophyll — the green fades and the yellow and orange pigments that were hidden underneath finally show. Reds are actually made fresh in some species, a chemical last hurrah before the leaf drops. 🍁", {"templ": False, "asks": ["why do leaves change color", "why do leaves turn orange", "why do leaves fall"], "keys": ["leaves", "autumn"]}),
    ("how to tie a tie", "The four-in-hand knot: loop the wide end over the narrow one, wrap around behind, bring the wide end across the front, up through the neck loop, and down through the knot you just formed. Tighten slowly while holding the knot. It takes four tries the first time and never leaves you after. 👔", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to tie a tie", "tie a tie"], "keys": ["tie"]}),
    ("how to cook rice", "For long-grain rice: 1 cup rice, 1.5–2 cups water, a pinch of salt; bring to a boil, cover, reduce to low for 15–18 minutes, then rest off the heat for 10. Do not stir while it cooks — the steam does the work. Fluff with a fork, not a spoon. 🍚", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how to cook rice", "how to make rice"], "keys": ["rice"]}),
    ("coffee", "Coffee works by blocking adenosine, the molecule that accumulates through the day and makes you feel sleepy — so caffeine does not add energy, it hides the tiredness until it wears off. The habit of coffee is a ritual on top of a drug whose tolerance builds within weeks. ☕", {"templ": False, "asks": ["how does coffee work", "does coffee give you energy", "is coffee good for you"], "keys": ["coffee", "caffeine"]}),
    ("why the sky is dark at night", "Olbers' paradox: in an infinite, eternal, everywhere-starred universe the night sky would be blindingly bright. The darkness tells us the universe has a finite age and is expanding — light from the most distant stars has not reached us yet, and its wavelengths have stretched into the infrared. 🌌", {"templ": False, "asks": ["why is the sky dark at night", "olbers paradox"], "keys": ["dark-sky"]}),
]
